## Decisions

### Should the thumbnail stay on `CONTENT` (one per set of bytes, as built), or move to `DOCUMENT` (one per place the bytes appear)?

**Background.** Documents are two tables. `CONTENT` holds the bytes: one row per SHA-256 and type, and the same bytes arriving twice are one row. `DOCUMENT` is one place those bytes appear: an email's part, a linked page, a year calendar. Several documents can hold the same `CONTENT`. A thumbnail is drawn only from the bytes: an image scaled down, a PDF's first page, or a page's `og:image`. So the same bytes always give the same picture. Issue #182 suggested `CONTENT` for that reason, and that's where I put it: `CONTENT.thumbnail` points to another `CONTENT` row holding a 480-pixel JPEG, and `CONTENT.thumbnailed` records when the worker finished.

**A real example from production.** One PNG, `cnt1ujIL9lQuyX` (177,681 bytes, filed as `image.png`), is a `part` of 7 different emails: documents `docFsDdULsmIWp`, `doc6qJPEvZtTpW`, `docmFU2rMWCWjX`, `docCje1w8le6Se`, `docAeOxDmlAG7v`, `docYjdDV0PdNnJ`, `docgdgUVbTdWGz`. This isn't rare: more than 50 images in production are each held by more than one document (school logos and newsletter banners). The two placements on this one image:

- (*) Keep it on CONTENT (as built)
  - The worker draws `cnt1ujIL9lQuyX` once and writes 1 thumbnail row plus 2 cells on `cnt1ujIL9lQuyX` (`thumbnail`, `thumbnailed`).
  - All 7 documents show the picture through `content.thumbnail`, served at `/api/blob/cnt1ujIL9lQuyX/thumbnail`. A reader sees it when they can read any of those 7 emails, through the new policy clause "the thumbnail of a document's bytes the viewer may see".
  - An eighth email carrying the same logo gets the thumbnail at once, with no new write.
  - Cost: for a page, a relative `og:image` (`/share/banner.png`) is resolved against the address of whichever document holding the page the worker happens to read first. Two documents holding the same HTML from different sites could disagree, but identical HTML at two addresses almost always names the same picture.
- ( ) Move it to DOCUMENT
  - Each of the 7 documents gets its own `thumbnail` cell: 7 cells plus 7 `thumbnailed` cells. They all point to the same thumbnail row, since identical JPEG bytes are still one `CONTENT` row.
  - The worker draws per document, or draws once and writes all 7. The eighth email waits for the worker to set its own cell.
  - It's served at `/api/blob/docFsDdULsmIWp/thumbnail` and read with the plain `document_visible` rule, so no new policy clause is needed.
  - A page's relative `og:image` is resolved against that document's own `url`.

I recommend keeping it on `CONTENT`. The picture depends only on the bytes, so there's one write per set of bytes instead of one per appearance (7 to 1 on this logo). The only per-document input, a relative `og:image` address, almost never differs between copies of the same page.

